1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,000
A render farm is the most expensive
machine in a studio.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,500
And nobody watches it in real time.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:15,000
When a job hangs overnight on eight GPUs,
the cost isn't the compute.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
It's the three shots that miss
the 9am review.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:27,000
This is a quarter of a billion rows of
frame-level telemetry in ClickHouse.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,500
198,000 jobs, 240 hosts — and it's still
being written to while we watch.

7
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:41,000
Three Gemini agents on Google Cloud ADK.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The Sentinel isn't given queries. It's
given the schema and a goal.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
It writes its own SQL through the
official ClickHouse MCP server.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,500
You're watching it compose those against
a quarter of a billion rows, live.

11
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:05,000
None of these statements are in our repo.

12
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:11,000
When it finds an out-of-memory failure,
it runs an ASOF join.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The last telemetry sample
before the job died.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
This one died five seconds after
the host hit 97% VRAM.

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,500
Two million rows touched out of
a quarter of a billion.

16
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Anywhere else, that's a window function
or a correlated subquery.

17
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:37,000
Error text is embedded with Vertex AI and
searched by cosine distance in ClickHouse.

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
It matches meaning, not keywords.

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So it finds the ticket from March
and the fix that closed it.

20
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:56,000
The Orchestrator weighs findings
against the dailies schedule.

21
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
A zombie on a show with a review in five
hours beats a bigger overrun due tomorrow.

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
When a proposal claims it protects a shot,
that claim is checked before it's written.

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,500
A shot that's actually on track gets
the claim dropped, not recorded.

24
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:20,000
The agent doesn't get to mark
its own homework.

25
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:25,000
Nothing has touched a render host.

26
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
A proposal is a record, and the number
only moves when a human approves it.

27
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,500
An agent finding waste
doesn't reduce your bill.

28
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:45,000
A supervisor approving the fix does.

29
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,000
Gemini, Google Cloud ADK,
ClickHouse through MCP.

30
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
This run cost about four cents

31
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,500
and found six thousand dollars
of capacity burning right now.

32
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,500
And the reviews it was about to cost.
